# C/C++ west-`const` on pointer types (local elaboration)

This is a **local** elaboration of shared **c-cpp-standards**. It is not yet in the upstream rulesbase; do not treat a missing upstream copy as permission to use east-`const`.


## Rule

**West-`const` is mandatory on all pointer types** in C and C++.

* Place `const` **before** the pointee type: `const T*`, not `T const*`.
* The same applies through cv layers on the pointee: prefer `const volatile T*` over `T const volatile*`.
* Pointer-to-`const` and const pointer remain distinct; west-`const` governs only where `const` qualifies the pointee:
  * `const T* p` — pointer to const `T` (required form);
  * `T* const p` — const pointer to `T` (unchanged; `const` after `*` is correct here);
  * `const T* const p` — const pointer to const `T` (required form for the pointee).


## Examples

```c
/* ✅ required */
const char* name;
const libver_result* result;
char* const fixed;              /* const pointer, non-const pointee */
const char* const both;

/* ❌ forbidden (east-const on the pointee) */
char const* name;
libver_result const* result;
char const* const both;
```


## Scope

* Applies to declarations, parameters, return types, casts, and typedefs involving pointers.
* Prefer matching existing project headers when they already use west-`const`.
* Bind `*` to the type (`const T* p`, not `const T *p`); see **local-c-cpp-local-declarators**.
* When upstream **c-cpp-standards** absorbs this rule, this local file may be removed after sync.
